From: Swift Linux <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] shm: label pixman buffers by their byte order

foot renders into PIXMAN_a8r8g8b8 and hands the buffer to the compositor
as WL_SHM_FORMAT_ARGB8888. pixman's format is a native-endian 32-bit
word, but wl_shm's formats are defined little-endian, so the two only
agree on little-endian hosts. On 32-bit PowerPC the compositor reads the
alpha byte as blue, and the default dark background comes out bright
blue.

On a big-endian host the bytes pixman writes are what wl_shm calls
BGRA8888, so label the buffer that way. wlroots' pixman renderer, the
one big-endian boards without a GPU composite with, offers it.
---
--- a/shm.c
+++ b/shm.c
@@ -995,7 +995,16 @@
               void (*release_cb)(struct buffer *buf, void *data), void *cb_data)
 {
     pixman_format_code_t pixman_fmt = PIXMAN_a8r8g8b8;
+#if __BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_BIG_ENDIAN__
+    /*
+     * pixman's a8r8g8b8 is a native-endian 32-bit word, while wl_shm's
+     * ARGB8888 is little-endian. On a big-endian host the bytes pixman
+     * writes are what wl_shm calls BGRA8888.
+     */
+    enum wl_shm_format shm_fmt = WL_SHM_FORMAT_BGRA8888;
+#else
     enum wl_shm_format shm_fmt = WL_SHM_FORMAT_ARGB8888;
+#endif
 
     static bool have_logged = false;
     static bool have_logged_10_fallback = false;
